{
  "concept": "In a right triangle, the names adjacent, opposite, and hypotenuse are roles defined relative to a chosen acute angle θ, and any two of those side lengths determine θ through an inverse trigonometric relation.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Right triangle in plain black outline, no axes or grid",
    "Horizontal base side from left vertex to lower-right vertex, labeled 'a' in italic below its midpoint",
    "Vertical side rising from the lower-right vertex to the apex, labeled 'p' in italic to the right of its midpoint",
    "Hypotenuse slanting from the apex down to the left vertex, labeled 'h' in italic above its midpoint",
    "Small open square at the lower-right vertex marking the 90° angle",
    "Single arc at the left vertex marking the acute angle, labeled with Greek letter 'θ'",
    "Role caption area (appears on click): text such as 'a — adjacent to θ', 'p — opposite θ', 'h — hypotenuse'",
    "Readout showing current θ in degrees and the three ratios p/h, a/h, p/a",
    "Highlight styling: selected side drawn thick in accent color with θ arc echoing the same color",
    "Relation banner showing the matching inverse form, e.g. θ = sin⁻¹(p/h), θ = cos⁻¹(a/h), θ = tan⁻¹(p/a)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "sideRole",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Each side name states a role relative to θ — clicking a, p, or h highlights that side together with the θ arc and names it adjacent, opposite, or hypotenuse, rather than left, up, or slanted."
    },
    {
      "id": "thetaSlider",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "Changing the acute angle reshapes the triangle while the labels keep their roles: as θ grows, the opposite side p lengthens relative to the adjacent side a, so the ratio p/a encodes the angle.",
      "label": "Acute angle θ (degrees)",
      "range": [5, 85, 1],
      "default": 30
    },
    {
      "id": "ratioInput",
      "type": "equation_input",
      "teaches": "Which two side lengths are known decides which inverse relation recovers θ — entering a ratio of sides sets the triangle's shape and lights up the matching sine, cosine, or tangent inverse.",
      "label": "Enter a known side ratio",
      "fields": [
        {
          "id": "ratioExpr",
          "label": "ratio =",
          "default": "p/h",
          "variables": ["p", "a", "h"],
          "domain": [0, 90],
          "role": "ratio selector — which pair of sides is known"
        },
        {
          "id": "ratioValue",
          "label": "value =",
          "default": "0.5",
          "variables": [],
          "domain": [0, 5],
          "role": "numeric value of that ratio, used to solve for θ"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The reference triangle",
      "narration": "A bare right triangle: base a, vertical side p, hypotenuse h, a square marking the right angle and an arc marking the acute angle θ. There are no numbers — this is a template, not a specific triangle.",
      "state": { "thetaSlider": 30 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Names are roles, not directions",
      "narration": "Click each label in turn. a lies against θ, so it is adjacent; p sits across from θ, so it is opposite; h stretches between them as the hypotenuse. Move θ to the other vertex in your mind and the names would swap.",
      "state": { "thetaSlider": 30 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Shallow angle",
      "narration": "Drag θ down to about 20°. The opposite side p becomes short compared with the adjacent side a, so p/a is small — a small ratio means a small angle.",
      "state": { "thetaSlider": 20 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Steep angle",
      "narration": "Push θ up to about 70°. Now p towers over a and the ratio p/a is large. Every value of θ gives one distinct set of ratios.",
      "state": { "thetaSlider": 70 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Running it backwards",
      "narration": "Type a known ratio — say p/h = 0.5 — and the triangle snaps to the angle that produces it, with θ = sin⁻¹(0.5) highlighted. Two measured sides are enough to recover the unknown angle.",
      "state": { "thetaSlider": 30 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Adjacent, opposite, and hypotenuse are roles assigned by θ, not fixed directions on the page — and because each angle produces its own unique set of side ratios, reading a ratio backwards through an inverse trig function recovers the angle.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Center a single black-outline right triangle on a white field: a long horizontal base, a shorter vertical right side, and a hypotenuse falling from the apex to the sharp left vertex, so the triangle is wide and low. Italic serif labels: 'a' centered below the base, 'p' outside the vertical side, 'h' above the midpoint of the hypotenuse, 'θ' just inside the left vertex next to a single thin arc. A small open square at the lower-right vertex marks the right angle. No axes, no grid, no shading, no numbers in the default view. On interaction, the selected side and the θ arc turn a single accent color (e.g. orange for opposite, blue for adjacent, green for hypotenuse) with a short caption naming its role; a small side panel shows θ in degrees and the three ratios, and a banner shows the matching inverse relation. When θ changes, keep the hypotenuse length fixed and the left vertex anchored so the triangle visibly rocks between wide-and-flat and narrow-and-tall while the labels stay attached to their sides."
}

